  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Wet and unsettled weather returns today into\nthis weekend. Continued above normal temperatures before cooler \nconditions arrive Sunday and beyond. Dry weather expected most \nof next week.\n\nHigh pressure well southeast of New England will maintain\nsoutherly winds into tonight. A slow-moving cold front\napproaches from the west today, then stalls across our region\nFriday while waves of low pressure travel along it. A final low\npressure is expected to move into the Maritimes Sunday, finally\nmoving this front offshore. \n\nThe greatest rainfall chances will be across western MA today.\nThe focus then shifts across RI and southeast MA tonight into\nSaturday. Relatively light rainfall anticipated today and\ntonight. The same cannot be said for Friday into Saturday \nmorning. The prolonged nature of this rainfall, as well as the\nwell above normal amounts of moisture, should lead to a\nsignificant rainfall. The latest guidance increased potential \nrainfall amounts yet again. However, there is only moderate\nconfidence in the location of the axis of heaviest rainfall.\nVariance in rainfall 48-hour amounts in the ensembles were\nbetween 0.75-1.75\". This is a signal that not all the details\nare refined quite yet. Further changes are possible with later\nforecasts.\n\nAll that said, our best estimate is for 0.50-1.00\" of rainfall \nacross the western half of southern New England through\nSaturday. Farther east, rainfall of 1.00-2.00\" is more likely.\nA narrow band of rainfall up to 3\" is possible. Again, the exact\nlocations this sets up is not yet known, but the most likely\narea for be from southern RI into southeast MA.\n\nFortunately, we've been very dry for a while and flash flood \nguidance is high. Still not expecting flash flooding to be an \nissue. Some poor drainage/urban flooding is possible, especially \ngiven the possibility of leaves blocking drainage this time of \nyear. There continues to be a marginal risk within the Excessive\nRainfall Outlook for Friday into Saturday.\n\nRain chances drop off Saturday morning but showers will continue to \nbe possible through Sunday morning with a cyclonic flow overhead; \nnothing like Friday/Friday night though. NW flow ushers in a colder, \nalbeit closer to normal, airmass for early next week with high \ntemperatures returning to the 50s.\n\nA series of fronts then move across southern New England next \nweek. However, limited moisture should only mean periods of\nclouds at times and mainly dry weather.",
